ID: B2-Ch05a-Ogres-heard
TEXT:

 

No sooner do I step into the corridor than muffled voices carry down the stone passage from around the far corner.

"They're rolling out the ogres for this wave," one guard says.

"Velgos's captives?" the second asks.

"The very ones. He's got half their clan locked up, threatening to slit their throats if the warriors don't fight on his leash. One of them is a female shaman. Rare sight, these days."

"A shaman? That explains the robes and the chalk markings on her face..."

"You saw her?"

"Guarding the pen right next to the locker rooms," the second guard chuckles. "Velgos had the boss shift them there so he could butcher one or two on short notice if the fighters stepped out of line."

"Hah! The brutes on the sand don't even know their kin are sitting right under their noses. If they were stupid enough to get ambushed, they can die as the troll's meat shields. Good show for the crowd either way."

Their boots clatter against the flagstones as their laughter fades into the lower corridors.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05a-Toast, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05a-Toast)


ID: B2-Ch05a-Toast
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch5_heard_guards == 1) {
 I hurry down the flagstones, scanning the heavy timber doors lining the corridor. A booming bellow of laughter echoes through one of the thick oak panels. I rap three times on the wood.

"Hadrik, you in there?"

"Hold your horses, give me a second!" Footsteps thud against stone, and the door swings wide.

Glass bottles clink in crates and on the floor behind him. Hadrik has enough dwarven brew to supply a tavern. His division-mates sit hunched against the far wall, pale and silent, completely ignoring the drinks.

"Congratulations on clearing the first wave, Barry!" Hadrik beams, his beard splitting in a wide grin. "Come to share a toast before the next round?"

"Not quite," I say. "I came to borrow a bottle of your dwarven ale, if you can spare one."

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_heard_guards == 0) {
 

I hurry down the flagstones, scanning the heavy timber doors lining the corridor. A booming bellow of laughter echoes through one of the thick oak panels. I rap three times on the wood.

"Hadrik, you in there?"

"Hold your horses, give me a second!" Footsteps thud against stone, and the door swings wide.

Glass bottles clink in crates and on the floor behind him. Hadrik has enough dwarven brew to supply a tavern. His division-mates sit hunched against the far wall, pale and silent, completely ignoring the drinks.

"Congratulations on clearing the first wave, Barry!" Hadrik beams, his beard splitting in a wide grin. "Come to share a toast before the next round?"

"Not quite," I say. "I came to borrow a bottle of your dwarven ale, if you can spare one."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch05a-Maliciously, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05a-Maliciously)


ID: B2-Ch05a-Maliciously
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_creatures == 1 && v_b2_ch4_beer == 0) {
 "Take whatever you want!" Hadrik waves an arm over the crates. "Brought all this for the lads, but not a single soul in this room appreciates a proper pre-battle drink. Help yourself, I've got plenty!"

"Much appreciated." I scoop up a thick glass bottle from the floor. "Now I just need a way onto the sands before the horns sound."

I glance around the threshold for a wall lever or a counterweight pulley on the iron gate.

Hadrik tilts his head. "You're heading out before the match starts?"

"That's the idea. Got a way to open this grate?"

"A lever? No." Hadrik cracks his knuckles and steps up to the iron bars. "I've got something better. Stand clear."

His fist drives forward in a blur. The iron gate tears straight out of its stone mountings with a deafening screech and hurtles out onto the arena sand in a cloud of mortar.

"Clear as day!" Hadrik grins.

"Perfect," I say. "Thanks."

I step through the shattered archway onto the sand and give him a wave over my shoulder.

"See you after the round."

"Uh... yeah. See you," Hadrik calls back, scratching his beard.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_creatures == 0 && v_b2_ch4_beer == 0) {
 "Take whatever you want!" Hadrik waves an arm over the crates. "Brought all this for the lads, but not a single soul in this room appreciates a proper pre-battle drink. Help yourself, I've got plenty!"

"Much appreciated." I scoop up a thick glass bottle from the floor.

I head straight through the gap in the wall where Hadrik's iron gate used to be before he punched it into the arena.

"See you after the round," I say, tossing a wave behind me.

"Uh... yeah. See you, I guess," Hadrik calls back, scratching his beard.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_creatures == 1 && v_b2_ch4_beer == 1) {
 "Take whatever you want!" Hadrik chuckles. "I did say I owed you a drink. Dwarven ale hits a little harder than tavern swill, but a debt's a debt! Grab whichever one catches your eye."

"Much appreciated." I scoop up a thick glass bottle from the floor. "Now I just need a way onto the sands before the horns sound."

I glance around the threshold for a wall lever or a counterweight pulley on the iron gate.

Hadrik tilts his head. "You're heading out before the match starts?"

"That's the idea. Got a way to open this grate?"

"A lever? No." Hadrik cracks his knuckles and steps up to the iron bars. "I've got something better. Stand clear."

His fist drives forward in a blur. The iron gate tears straight out of its stone mountings with a deafening screech and hurtles out onto the arena sand in a cloud of mortar.

"Clear as day!" Hadrik grins.

"Perfect," I say. "Thanks."

I step through the shattered archway onto the sand and give him a wave over my shoulder.

"See you after the round."

"Uh... yeah. See you," Hadrik calls back, scratching his beard.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_creatures == 0 && v_b2_ch4_beer == 1) {
 "Take whatever you want!" Hadrik chuckles. "I did say I owed you a drink. Dwarven ale hits a little harder than tavern swill, but a debt's a debt! Grab whichever one catches your eye."

"Much appreciated." I scoop up a thick glass bottle from the floor.

I head straight through the gap in the wall where Hadrik's iron gate used to be before he punched it into the arena.

"See you after the round," I say, tossing a wave behind me.

"Uh... yeah. See you, I guess," Hadrik calls back, scratching his beard.

}
 I stride out onto the open sand, ale bottle swinging in my right hand. High above, the announcer leans over the edge of his box and begins screaming into his horn.

"What do you think you're doing down there? The round has not begun! Return to your division staging area this instant! Do you hear me? Get back in your pen! Hey, are you deaf?"

I tune out the screeching and keep my eyes locked on the center of the arena. Velgos stops his victory circuit and turns toward me, yellow tusks bared in a broad, ugly sneer.

"Come to beg for your miserable life, human?" the troll's voice booms across the sand. "Save your breath. Your death was decided the moment you stepped into this pit!"

I say nothing. My boots crunch steadily over the grit.

"Curse it all!" the announcer shrieks up in the box. "Open the gates! Open all of them right now!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05a-Flammable, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05a-Flammable)


ID: B2-Ch05a-Flammable
TEXT:

 A brass horn blares across the coliseum, and the iron portcullis of my division groans upward. I deliberately drag my pace across the sand, keeping my stride measured enough for Leila to close the distance behind me.

Velgos keeps barking taunts. He thinks I brought him an offering. Clearly the troll has never encountered Northern dwarven spirits. Considering the throat-stripping fire that hit my chest the first time Hadrik handed me a cup, this concoction is closer to lamp oil than beverage. If Velgos knew the proof inside this glass, he would not be standing there grinning.

"Trying to bribe me, little human?" Velgos laughs, the sound scraping like grindstones. "A flask of swill won't buy your skin. Why not drop to your knees instead? Let forty thousand people watch you crawl before I take your head!"

I halt ten paces out and offer a polite, tight smile, lifting the heavy bottle toward him. Leila is twenty feet back now, moving with light, rapid steps, the rest of our division trailing behind her. She keeps her arms tucked, the burning pitch of the torch hidden behind her back.

"Cheers," I say, and raise the bottle high.

"What are you trying to—"

I snap my arm forward and shatter the thick glass across the bridge of his snout. 

Amber liquid drenches his forehead, tusks, and leather-and-steel breastplate. Velgos stumbles back in confusion, wiping stinging alcohol out of his eyes.

"Leila, now!" I shout, springing backward.

The torch arcs through the air. The moment the burning head clips the troll's chest, the dwarven spirits detonate in a violent sheet of yellow fire.

Velgos shrieks, batting frantically at his blazing skull with both hands. Leila darts into the smoke like a striking snake, her enchanted dagger flashing in a clean silver arc that shears the left pauldron completely off his shoulder. Velgos snarls, blindly drawing both curved scimitars to sweep the air in front of him, but Leila is already bounding backward out of reach.

"I will butcher you!" the troll roars through the thinning smoke. "I will chop you into scraps so small your kin will bury an empty sack!"

His yellow eyes burn with feral fury. The flames across his torso gutter and die, and the blackened skin beneath begins knitting back together almost instantly. Normal fire is not going to overcome regeneration this aggressive. The clock is running. If I do not make a move right now, Velgos will dictate the pace. Do I strike while his shoulder is exposed, play decoy for Leila, or fall back to regroup with the team?

choice("Try to cut the troll's head off", B2-Ch05a-Cut, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05a-Cut, v_b2_ch5_first_cut = 0)
choice("Try to act as a decoy, so Leila can cut the troll's head off", B2-Ch05a-Decoy, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05a-Decoy, v_b2_ch5_first_cut = 0)
choice("Go for a tactical retreat.", B2-Ch05a-Tactical, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05a-Tactical, v_b2_ch5_first_cut = 0)


ID: B2-Ch05a-Cut
TEXT:

set(v_b2_ch5_first_cut,1) if (v_agility >= 3 && v_strength >= 2 || v_combat_technique >= 2 && v_strength >= 2)
set(v_b2_ch5_first_cut,0) if (v_b2_ch5_heard_guards == 0 && v_is_dead == 0 || v_agility < 3 && v_combat_technique < 2 || v_strength < 2)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_agility >= 3 || v_combat_technique >= 2)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_agility < 3 && v_combat_technique < 2)
#if(v_agility >= 3 && v_strength >= 2) {
 

The bare shoulder gives me the line I need. I surge forward, closing the distance before the troll can reset his stance, and drive Daren's enchanted dagger across the exposed flesh of his neck with everything behind my shoulders. 

The blade cuts through muscle and spine in one clean, shearing sweep. Velgos's head tumbles free.

I snag the topknot of coarse hair before the head hits the sand and fling it forty paces away toward the outer wall. The headless bulk of the troll stumbles backward, broad hands clawing at empty air.

"Leila, finish it!" I yell, stepping back. "While he's down—"

The words catch in my throat.

At the severed stump of the neck, gray flesh bubbles and weaves into new muscle. Sinew stretches, bone knits, and in five seconds flat a brand-new head sits atop his shoulders, gasping and contorted in agony.

"I will carve you into pieces," Velgos rasps, his new vocal cords thick with phlegm and pain. "Slowly. While your friends watch."

"Yeah, yeah," I say. "Heard it all before."

}
#if(v_agility >= 3 && v_strength < 2) {
 

The bare shoulder gives me the line I need. I surge forward, closing the distance before the troll can reset his stance, and drive my enchanted dagger across the exposed flesh of his neck. 

The edge bites deep into meat and gristle, but my arm lacks the raw force to carry the stroke through the spine. The blade jams halfway through his throat. Velgos roars and brings a scimitar down in a vicious cleave aimed at my shoulder. I wrench my dagger free and throw myself backward just as the steel whistling through the space I occupied. Within seconds, the crimson gash across his neck closes seamlessly.

"You'll need more than that to put me in the dirt," Velgos growls.

}
#if(v_combat_technique >= 2 && v_strength >= 2 && v_agility < 3) {
 

I lunge forward, feinting a straight thrust at his sternum. Velgos bites on the misdirection, sweeping his heavy scimitar across to bat my blade aside. I pivot on my back foot, duck beneath the steel arc, and come up hard on his unprotected left side, driving my enchanted dagger through his neck in a single, fluid strike.

The severed head drops into my off-hand. I heave it by the hair toward the outer barrier wall. The headless torso reels backward on the sand.

"Leila, finish it!" I yell. "While he's down—"

The words catch in my throat.

At the severed stump of the neck, gray flesh bubbles and weaves into new muscle. Sinew stretches, bone knits, and in five seconds flat a brand-new head sits atop his shoulders, gasping and contorted in agony.

"I will carve you into pieces," Velgos rasps, his new vocal cords thick with phlegm and pain. "Slowly. While your friends watch."

"Yeah, yeah," I say. "Heard it all before."

}
#if(v_combat_technique >= 2 && v_agility < 3 && v_strength < 2) {
 

I lunge forward, feinting a straight thrust at his sternum. Velgos bites on the misdirection, sweeping his heavy scimitar across to bat my blade aside. I pivot on my back foot, duck beneath the steel arc, and come up on his unprotected left side to slash at his throat. 

The edge bites deep, but without enough raw power behind the swing, the blade catches on bone. Velgos snarls and brings his second scimitar around in a lethal backhand. I rip the knife loose and spring backward, barely clearing the whistling edge. By the time I regain my footing, the wound on his throat has already sealed shut.

"You'll need more than that to put me in the dirt," Velgos growls.

}
#if(v_agility < 3 && v_combat_technique < 2) {
 

I charge the exposed shoulder, aiming straight for the troll's neck. Velgos reads the telegraphed rush and brings his right scimitar whistling down. I throw myself aside to clear the blade and push forward to deliver the kill stroke. 

The first swing was bait. As I commit my weight forward, his left scimitar whips across from below in a blinding counter-stroke. The edge catches me across the neck before I can even begin to pull away. Everything goes black before my body hits the sand.

}
#if(v_agility >= 3 || v_combat_technique >= 2) {
 "Behind you! Heads up!" Bruce roars from the rear.

A pair of massive, slate-gray ogres thunder through the gate behind Velgos, brandishing iron-banded tree trunks for clubs. Leila and I spring backward, falling in with the rest of our division as the line forms up on the sand.

"I knew it," the fire mage whimpers, gripping his staff with white knuckles. "I knew they wouldn't let us fight just the troll. They had to throw ogres into the pen!"

"Quit whining," Bruce barks, cracking his knuckles. "Ogres are big, dumb, and slow. I can snap both their necks myself."

}
